+| 14 | `src/app/api/srs/review/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | `SrsService.gradeCard` reads and returns a graded card payload including front/back/reading/meaning and scheduling state. Route and service guard precede the card lookup. |
+| 15 | `src/app/api/srs/sessions/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | GET returns session history/resumable data, queue counts and daily-new counts; POST plans a queue and returns session state. Both route methods and `SessionService` entry points are gated. |
+| 16 | `src/app/api/srs/sessions/[id]/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | GET returns current card, rating previews and recent answer fronts. Route plus `getSessionState`/hydration entry points are guarded. The PATCH action is not a content read; the file’s GET is the exposed read surface. |
+| 17 | `src/app/api/srs/sessions/[id]/answer/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | `SessionService.answer` grades, hydrates the answered front and next card. Route and service guards precede session/card reads. |
+| 18 | `src/app/api/srs/sessions/[id]/undo/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | Undo restores a card and returns current card/session state. Route and `SessionService.undo` are guarded before review/card hydration. |
+| 19 | `src/app/api/srs/sessions/[id]/summary/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | Summary derives from review/session rows and may hydrate recent card fronts. Route and `getSummary`/read helpers are guarded. |
+| 20 | `src/app/api/srs/stats/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | `SrsService.getStats` returns card totals, forecast and recent review fronts. Route and direct service guard precede all queries. |
+| 21 | `src/app/api/srs/sync/pull/route.ts` | **PROTECTED** | `SyncService.pull` returns deck metadata, card deltas (front/back/reading/meaning/source fields and scheduler state) and review deltas. Route and direct `pull`/`snapshot` guards precede database reads, mapping and sync-log writes. |
+| 22 | `src/app/api/srs/sync/route.ts` (`GET` status) | **NO_KANJI_EXPOSURE** | `SyncService.getStatus` selects device roster, registry status, sync-log metadata/messages, and user-wide aggregate totals. It does not query or serialize card/deck/question text, character, reading, meaning, card type or source reference; counts are generic user sync/review totals, not a Kanji-specific count. A route-level regression invokes the actual handler with a controlled status fixture and asserts the response shape contains no Kanji/card fields. No Kanji gate was added to this path. Other sync verbs (device registration/revocation) are mutation/status operations, not the listed GET read path. |
+
+**Totals:** 21 `PROTECTED`, 1 `NO_KANJI_EXPOSURE`, 0 `REQUIRES_GUARD`, 0 `UNVERIFIED` for the exact 22 read paths. The protected route group is exercised through actual route handlers with no-store denial assertions, and the direct service group is exercised independently. No Kanji-bearing success payload was fabricated.
+
+## 2. MindTree authorization and public/editorial split
+
+The raw helper names from K0.7 are now internal-review boundaries:
+
+- `listMindTreeCatalog()` — awaits `requirePermission("cms.read")`, then checks all catalog source operations before building/returning rows.
+- `getMindTreeEntry(character)` — awaits `requirePermission("cms.read")`, then checks the source set for the composite entry, including the derived learning-graph source, before building/returning it.
+- `listMindTreeEditorialContext()` — awaits `requirePermission("cms.read")`, then checks KANJIDIC2, KanjiVG and first-party source operations before returning source/editorial context.
+
+All three fail closed with no privilege parameter or caller context argument. CMS read authorization is not a rights or publication grant. Current K0.5A states include conditional/blocked and unknown provenance, so an authenticated editor successfully passes `cms.read` and then receives the independent `[RIGHTS_GATE]` denial before the helper returns content. The unknown derived source in the detailed entry remains blocked until its provenance is registered and adjudicated.
+
+Learner delivery is separate. Public pages now call `listPublicMindTreeCatalog()` / `getPublicMindTreeEntry(character)`, which use the existing public rights-and-membership decision, not CMS privilege. The public source set was expanded to include both first-party source families represented by the composite entry; this adds a check only and does not change their rights state. The character page evaluates the per-character membership decision, the bulk catalog requires a bulk decision, and both public MindTree pages are force-dynamic so policy is re-evaluated rather than serving a prerendered response. The development studio calls the authorized editorial helper and maps authentication/rights denial to `notFound()` without rendering source details.
+
+`getMindTreeEditorialVisualPreview` remains the separately gated visual-preview helper recorded in historical K0.7; it was not renamed or treated as one of these three raw catalog/context helpers.
+
+### Authorization and editorial regression coverage
+
+`tests/kanji-read-path-closure-k07a.test.ts` covers all three raw helpers for unauthenticated 401, authenticated learner/forged-role 403, and editor `cms.read` followed by the expected independent source-rights denial. Empty/malformed actor identity remains 401. The same suite verifies that admin/CMS privilege cannot override the public delivery gate; draft `明` remains unpublished, unsupported `龍` remains missing, and both known conditional and unregistered source provenance deny. It also exercises every protected route before body parsing/query and checks the generic no-store 451 shape.
+
+Existing M4–M9 data-model tests use explicit test-only policy stubs to test their serialization/editorial model fixtures; those stubs are not evidence of rights clearance or authorization. The K0.7A auth suite uses the real permission resolver and real immutable rights gate.
+
+## 3. Cache, serialization and alternate-path review
+
+- All gated route adapters use `withKanjiPublicDeliveryGate`: denial occurs before the handler; denial is a generic no-store 451; if delivery is authorized in a future valid deployment, the resulting response is still marked `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+- All affected API routes remain `force-dynamic`. The MindTree catalog/detail pages are also force-dynamic; module-level MindTree indexes cannot bypass the per-call public/CMS guard before a serializer returns them.
+- SRS and JLPT reviewed services have per-call local maps for joins/hydration, but no persistent result cache that can bypass these top-level guards. `SessionService.hydrateCards` is private and only reached through guarded methods. `SyncService.pull` maps DB rows only after its direct guard.
+- The pre-existing K0.7 stale-cache sentinel regression injects a restricted value into `KanjiLexicalGraphService`’s MindTree cache and verifies the current denied read does not return it. K0.7 also tests the alternate unified-search and retrieval boundaries; those results remain part of the historical validation and were rerun here.
+- No new Kanji search serializer was found in the 22-path set. JLPT test/session serializers and SRS card/queue/session/stat/sync-pull serializers are all gated at both route and applicable direct service boundaries. The SRS sync-status exception was shape-tested as above.
+
+### Rights/editorial cache-transition limitation
+
+A genuine **allowed → restricted** rights/editorial transition through the normal production read paths is not representable in the current architecture without an unauthorized policy mutation or a mocked policy bypass:
+
+1. `rightsEnforcement` reads the immutable `K0_5A_INITIAL_RIGHTS_HISTORY`; there is no production history-provider or test-only service injection seam.
+2. The membership ledger is the empty `KANJI_MEMBERSHIP_BASELINE`, and `membershipEvidenceVerified` is hard-coded false; there is no authenticated publication-ledger transition path.
+3. No database-backed rights ledger exists in this scope, and using a fabricated `CLEARED` event, mutable caller flag, or mocked allow-state to preload a normal read cache would falsely imply clearance. No such fixture or bypass was added.
+
+Therefore the requested end-to-end rights/editorial cache-transition test is **genuinely untestable** under the allowed constraints. The current restricted-state stale-cache sentinel test, per-call pre-read gates, dynamic routes and no-store headers demonstrate fail-closed behavior at the available boundary, but do not claim a simulated state transition or full cache invalidation proof. Before a future owner-authorized rights/publication state transition, add an authoritative server-side provider and a non-caller-controlled test seam, then exercise the real normal read path and each serializer. This limitation is why the verdict is conditional rather than unconditional PASS.
+
