+## 3. Model, workflow and data flow
+
+### Canonical source
+
+K0.9 extends the existing K0.5A field-provenance contract, K0.5B rights policy, Mind Tree graph model and versioned CMS service. It uses existing CMS `kanji` content items with `entityId: null`, JSON payloads, immutable version snapshots and CMS audit events. Identity, field content, provenance, rights, editorial review, membership and delivery are kept separate. No new table or migration is needed for this payload-backed boundary.
+
+After initialization, the versioned CMS item and its audit/version history are the canonical authoring source. `src/data/kanji-foundation/provisional-draft-shells.json` is only a deterministic bootstrap-intent file. It contains the five exact identities `明`, `休`, `道`, `新`, `話`, with empty `fields`, unassessed variant relationships, candidate membership, no source assertions and no named creator. **No CMS bootstrap was executed**, so these remain seed shells rather than persisted CMS records.
+
+Field revisions are immutable in CMS history and have content/revision digests, contributor attribution, exact source assertions, creation method, rights history and editorial review evidence. Review verification is performed by an authenticated admin distinct from the item author; the review is bound to the exact editorial revision digest and must be represented in CMS versions and audit. CMS approval and the later publication event are separate. Updating a draft creates a new immutable CMS version; edits after approval/publication create a superseding revision rather than overwriting the approved value.
+
+K0.9 membership uses an append-only event stream per identity and goal. Candidate, approved, published, excluded, deferred and withdrawn outcomes are distinct; the four goals remain independent. Publication eligibility uses each goal’s latest event so a withdrawn goal cannot remain publicly eligible through stale history.
+
+Rights events are scoped to a field’s attached source refs. First-party clearance requires owner authorization evidence. Third-party clearance requires pinned primary-license evidence and a separate qualified review; existing source blocks are still enforced. No corpus-wide clearance prerequisite was added.
+
+### Mind Tree and stroke data
+
+The graph supports multiple decompositions, explicit component roles (`radical`, `visual_component`, `semantic_component`, `phonetic_component`), positions, sourced edges and candidate/editorially-reviewed graph states. Role claims are explicit and never inferred from visual resemblance. Public projection emits sanitized relationship labels only after the field and every source pass their gates. The K0.9 learner page contains no synthetic stroke geometry and explicitly states that authentic stroke order is unavailable; the existing synthetic demo remains synthetic.
+
+### Deterministic pipeline
+
+`scripts/build-kanji-foundation.ts` consumes a versioned authorized CMS export, validates the immutable K0.5A baseline, source and field provenance, exact editorial/CMS review history, rights, membership and explicit output field keys, then writes a deterministic JSON projection. Output defaults to a fixed build timestamp for reproducibility. The CLI also emits a deterministic internal rejection report containing item IDs and reason codes on stdout; the report is kept separate from the learner-facing artifact. Writes inside the repo are allowed only under ignored build-output directories; this verification used a temporary directory outside the repository.
+
+The public projection is an explicit allowlist: identity ID, exact character, code point, and eligible `meaning`, `reading.on`, `reading.kun`, `mnemonic`, `vocabulary` and `decomposition` values. It never serializes CMS records, source assertions, private notes, reviewer identity, audit details, or rights rationale.
